#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# material_shading

#% text
# `material_shading(name)` is a directive of 3D graphics that names a material for the faces that follow.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The 3D renderer shades faces with its own fixed light; `material_shading` is accepted in graphics and plot options, so code written for other lighting runs, and is not drawn.

#% item
# The call stays as it is when evaluated alone.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
graphics3d([material_shading("glazed"), sphere()])
#% output
#= image("material_shading.files/material_shading-1.svgz")

#% cell
plot3d(x*y, [x, -1, 1], [y, -1, 1], plot_style: [material_shading("matte")])
#% output
#= image("material_shading.files/material_shading-2.svgz")

#% cell
material_shading("glazed")
#% output
#= material_shading("glazed")

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The picture is the same without it:

#% cell
graphics3d([orange, sphere()])
#% output
#= image("material_shading.files/material_shading-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `graphics3d`, `plot3d`, `lighting`, `toon_shading`
